{
 "lead": "A second-order system is the smallest system that can overshoot and ring, and its whole response is fixed by two numbers. ωn, the undamped natural frequency in rad/s, sets the speed. ζ, the damping ratio, a pure number, sets the shape. Servo motor datasheets, hard-disk head positioners and drone attitude loops quote these two numbers because they predict overshoot long before a wire is connected. This chapter writes the standard form, sorts the response into four damping cases, derives the step response of each, and reads ζ and ωn off the s-plane.",
 "prereq": "You need: <a href=\"#standard-test-signals\">Chapter 2.1</a> for the step input, <a href=\"#type-and-order-of-a-system\">Chapter 2.2</a> for the standard second-order form, and school-level quadratic roots and trigonometry.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Standard form and the two numbers",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Divide numerator and denominator by the coefficient of s². If the DC gain is 1, the result is exactly the template below; if the gain is K, write K times the template and carry K separately."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "G(s) = ωn² / (s² + 2ζωn s + ωn²)",
     "where": "G(s) is the transfer function, output over input; ωn is the undamped natural frequency in rad/s; ζ (zeta) is the damping ratio, unitless; s is the Laplace variable in rad/s."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Read the two numbers straight off the denominator. The constant term is ωn². The coefficient of s is 2ζωn, with the 2. At s = 0 the template gives G(0) = 1, so a unit step settles at 1. If your gain is not 1, carry that gain separately."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The two numbers answer two different questions. ωn is how fast the system would ring if all friction vanished. ζ is how much that ringing is eaten away. Neither is a component value; both come from the denominator alone."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "s₁, s₂ = −ζωn ± ωn√(ζ² − 1) = −ζωn ± jωn√(1 − ζ²)",
     "where": "s₁, s₂ are the closed-loop poles in rad/s; the second form is used when ζ ≤ 1. The real part −ζωn is the same either way."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "For ζ < 1 the square root is imaginary and the poles form a complex pair. For ζ ≥ 1 the poles are real. That sign is the whole classification."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Four cases, four shapes",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The characteristic equation s² + 2ζωn s + ωn² = 0 has discriminant 4ωn²(ζ² − 1). Its sign sorts the response into four families."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "ζ",
      "Name",
      "Poles",
      "Step response c(t)"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "0",
       "Undamped",
       "±jωn",
       "1 − cos ωn t"
      ],
      [
       "0 < ζ < 1",
       "Underdamped",
       "−ζωn ± jωd",
       "1 − (1/√(1−ζ²)) e^(−ζωn t) sin(ωd t + φ)"
      ],
      [
       "1",
       "Critically damped",
       "−ωn, −ωn",
       "1 − (1 + ωn t) e^(−ωn t)"
      ],
      [
       "ζ > 1",
       "Overdamped",
       "s₁, s₂ real",
       "1 + (ωn/(2√(ζ²−1))) (e^(s₁t)/s₁ − e^(s₂t)/s₂)"
      ]
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Each row comes from the same partial-fraction start. For a unit step R(s) = 1/s, so C(s) = ωn²/[s(s² + 2ζωn s + ωn²)]. Take the underdamped case, the one every exam asks."
    },
    {
     "type": "steps",
     "items": [
      "Factor: C(s) = ωn²/[s(s − s₁)(s − s₂)], with s₁,₂ = −ζωn ± jωd and ωd = ωn√(1 − ζ²).",
      "Split into A/s + (Bs + C)/(s² + 2ζωn s + ωn²). Solving gives A = 1, so the final value is 1.",
      "Invert each term: c(t) = 1 − e^(−ζωn t)[cos ωd t + (ζ/√(1−ζ²)) sin ωd t].",
      "Fold the bracket into a single sine with φ = cos⁻¹ζ: c(t) = 1 − (1/√(1−ζ²)) e^(−ζωn t) sin(ωd t + φ)."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Read that line in three pieces. The 1 is the final value. The exponential e^(−ζωn t) shrinks the swing, with time constant 1/(ζωn). The sine at ωd swings the output back and forth. Raise ζ and the decay is faster and the first overshoot smaller, until at ζ = 1 the sine vanishes and the curve touches 1 from below without crossing it. At ζ = 0 nothing decays: c(t) = 1 − cos ωn t swings between 0 and 2 forever. Above ζ = 1 the two real poles separate, one fast and one slow, and the slow pole dominates."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Poles on a circle",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Hold ωn fixed and let ζ change. Every pole stays at distance ωn from the origin, so it slides along a circle of radius ωn."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "|s| = ωn,  θ = cos⁻¹ ζ,  ωd = ωn√(1 − ζ²)",
     "where": "|s| is the distance of a pole from the origin in rad/s; θ is the angle of the pole measured from the negative real axis, in radians; ωd is the damped frequency in rad/s."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "ζ = 1 puts both poles on the negative real axis at −ωn, so θ = 0. ζ = 0 puts them on the imaginary axis at θ = π/2 rad (90°). A pole at θ = π/4 rad (45°) gives ζ = cos 45° = 0.707. The vertical height of the pole above the real axis is exactly ωd, the frequency you would measure on an oscilloscope."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Two knobs, two effects. Changing ωn scales the circle: the poles move radially and the whole response speeds up or slows down, because time is measured in units of 1/ωn. Changing ζ slides the poles around the circle: toward the real axis means less overshoot and faster settling, toward the imaginary axis means more ringing. This is what moving a pole in the s-plane means in practice."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "An extra real pole far to the left, or a zero, bends this picture. Only a few universities examine that approximation here; treat the two-pole result above as the baseline."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
