 "traps": [
  "Forgetting the 2: the coefficient of s is 2ζωn, not ζωn. Half the wrong answers in this topic come from that slip.",
  "Normalise first. ωn = √(constant term) only after the s² coefficient is 1; with 4s² + 3s + 9 you must divide everything by 4 before matching.",
  "The underdamped formulas need √(1 − ζ²) real, so check ζ < 1 first. Plugging ζ = 1.5 into the sine form gives a nonsense imaginary frequency.",
  "Angles inside exponentials and sines are in radians. πζ/√(1−ζ²) and ωd t are radian quantities; a calculator left in degrees gives a wrong overshoot.",
  "Mp is a fraction of the final value. Report it as 9.48%, not as 0.0948, and remember it depends only on ζ, never on ωn."
 ],
 "quiz": [
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "Define ωn and ζ, and give the unit of each.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "ωn is the undamped natural frequency, in rad/s: the frequency at which the system would oscillate with zero damping. ζ is the damping ratio, unitless. In the standard form the constant term is ωn² and the coefficient of s is 2ζωn."
  },
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "List the four damping classes with the ζ range for each.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "ζ = 0 undamped; ζ between 0 and 1 (both ends excluded) underdamped; ζ = 1 critically damped; ζ greater than 1 overdamped."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Why does the underdamped step response always settle at 1, whatever ζ is?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "Because G(0) = ωn²/ωn² = 1. In the partial fractions the coefficient of 1/s is A = ωn²/(s₁s₂) = ωn²/ωn² = 1, and that term is the steady value. ζ only shapes the transient terms, which die away."
  },
