 "beyond": "Not for the exam. In industry a real machine is often measured, not derived: strike it with a step, read the ratio of two successive peaks, and the logarithmic decrement gives ζ directly. Practising engineers aim near ζ = 0.7 for a fast rise with only about 5% overshoot. A different rule of thumb, quarter-amplitude damping, is the setting where each peak is one quarter of the peak before it, and that happens near ζ ≈ 0.22. A higher-order plant is usually reduced to an equivalent second-order model by keeping the dominant pole pair, and that reduction is what makes these two numbers useful on a real servo axis. Search next for dominant pole approximation, logarithmic decrement, and quarter-amplitude damping."
